{"caption":"Photos by @joelsartore | Isn’t Dumpling just the cutest?

With short, stubby legs, it isn’t easy for striped skunks to outrun trouble, so they’ve developed a unique defense system to protect themselves from predators. When cornered, a skunk will first try to frighten the predator by turning its back, raising its tail, and arching its back while stomping its feet. If this display proves unsuccessful, the skunk will resort to spraying the animal with a strong-smelling fluid that can sting the eyes of the predator, giving the skunk just enough time to escape.

Photos taken @savannah_wrc_. To see more species featured in the Photo Ark, follow me @joelsartore. #PhotoArk","likes":239484,"comments":289,"photoCount":2,"videoCoverCount":0}

{"caption":"Women in this Sardinian mountain village have high odds of living to 100. Researchers have identified it as a new blue zone—a geographic region where residents aged 70 and older have exceptional rates of survival and longevity. Here, women who reach 70 are three times as likely to reach the century mark as their U.S. counterparts, so researchers are investigating whether factors such as diet, family life, and genetics could help explain that advantage.

Discover the clues to living a longer, healthier life at the link in bio.

Photograph by Marisa Montibeller","likes":54846,"comments":407,"photoCount":1,"videoCoverCount":0}

